self_contained_problem:
Consider magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) turbulence in a plasma that carries a strong mean (DC) magnetic field $\mathbf{B}_0$, such as the solar wind. Let $\delta\mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x},t)$ denote the fluctuating magnetic field, $\delta\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x},t)$ the fluctuating velocity field, $\delta n(\mathbf{x},t)$ the density fluctuations, and $\delta|\mathbf{B}|(\mathbf{x},t)$ the fluctuations of the magnetic-field magnitude. For each fluctuating field $f$, define the one-dimensional (reduced) power spectrum $E_f(k)$ in the inertial range so that $\langle f^2\rangle = \int E_f(k)\, dk$.

Two classical dimensional predictions for the inertial-range scaling are:
(i) the Kolmogorov scaling $E(k)\propto k^{-5/3}$, derived under the assumption of incompressible, isotropic, hydrodynamic turbulence with a constant energy cascade rate;
(ii) the Iroshnikov–Kraichnan scaling $E(k)\propto k^{-3/2}$, derived for Alfvénic turbulence in which counter-propagating Alfvén-wave packets interact weakly in the presence of a strong mean field $\mathbf{B}_0$.

Observations of solar-wind turbulence routinely report:
(a) magnetic and velocity power spectra with slopes close to $-5/3$ in the MHD inertial range, despite the presence of a strong $\mathbf{B}_0$ that would naively favor $-3/2$;
(b) density power spectra $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and magnetic-magnitude spectra $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ that also exhibit slopes near $-5/3$;
(c) a flattening of $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ at high frequencies (small scales) approaching the ion-cyclotron / ion-gyroradius dissipation range, while the spectra of vector components of $\delta\mathbf{B}$ and $\delta\mathbf{v}$ instead steepen near the same range.

Provide a first-principles theoretical explanation, derived from the equations of compressible MHD (or an appropriate kinetic extension), that accounts simultaneously for: 
1. Why $E_{\delta\mathbf{B}}(k)$ and $E_{\delta\mathbf{v}}(k)$ display a $-5/3$ rather than a $-3/2$ inertial-range slope in the presence of a strong mean magnetic field;
2. Why the compressive spectra $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ also follow a $-5/3$ slope;
3. Why $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ flatten near the ion-cyclotron dissipation scale while vector magnetic and velocity component spectra steepen.

Consider a charged test particle of charge $q$, mass $m$, and velocity $\mathbf v$ moving in a magnetic field of the form $\mathbf B(\mathbf x,t) = \mathbf B_0 + \mathbf b(\mathbf x,t)$, where $\mathbf B_0 = B_0\hat{\mathbf z}$ is a uniform background field representative of the interplanetary magnetic field and $\mathbf b$ is a zero-mean, statistically stationary, three-dimensional random turbulent fluctuation with prescribed power spectrum and correlation tensor (allowing components such as slab Alfvénic fluctuations $\mathbf b(z,t)$ and quasi-two-dimensional fluctuations $\mathbf b(x,y)$ transverse to $\mathbf B_0$). The particle obeys the Lorentz equation $m\,d\mathbf v/dt = q\,\mathbf v\times\mathbf B$, and one defines the pitch angle $\mu = \cos\theta = v_\parallel/v$, the parallel mean free path $\lambda_\parallel$, and the perpendicular diffusion coefficient $\kappa_\perp = \lim_{t\to\infty}\langle(\Delta\mathbf x_\perp)^2\rangle/(2t)$ for an ensemble of particles. Magnetic field lines themselves satisfy $d\mathbf x/ds = \mathbf B/|\mathbf B|$, with field-line diffusion coefficient $D_{FL} = \lim_{s\to\infty}\langle(\Delta\mathbf x_\perp)^2\rangle/(2s)$.

Determine, as functionals of the three-dimensional structure (spectrum, anisotropy, and geometry, including slab versus quasi-2D content) of $\mathbf b$:

(a) the pitch-angle scattering coefficient $D_{\mu\mu}(\mu)$ and resulting parallel mean free path $\lambda_\parallel$;

(b) the transverse (perpendicular) spatial diffusion coefficient $\kappa_\perp$ of energetic particles across $\mathbf B_0$;

(c) the stochastic field-line diffusion coefficient $D_{FL}$, and its quantitative relation to $\kappa_\perp$;

(d) the conditions on the turbulence under which solar energetic particles released from a localized source (e.g., a flare or coronal mass ejection) at the inner heliosphere reach a distant observer in a given direction;

(e) a quantitative explanation, in terms of the polarity reversal of $\mathbf B_0$ across the heliospheric current sheet on a 22-year cycle, of the observed 22-year modulation of the galactic cosmic-ray intensity at 1 AU.

Provide a unified analytical or rigorously justified transport theory predicting (a)–(e) from the statistics of $\mathbf b$.

Consider the solar wind modeled as a magnetized plasma flowing radially outward from the Sun through the heliosphere. Within this flow, transverse magnetic and velocity fluctuations satisfying the Alfvén relation (correlated b and v perturbations propagating along the mean magnetic field at the Alfvén speed) are observed in the inner heliosphere to be predominantly outward-propagating with high normalized cross helicity. As heliocentric distance increases, these outward-propagating Alfvénic fluctuations are observed to lose their Alfvénic character (normalized cross helicity decreases, residual energy becomes more negative, and the magnetic-to-kinetic energy ratio departs from unity) and eventually to be replaced by more isotropic, fully developed MHD turbulence in the outer heliosphere.

Within the framework of compressible MHD describing such a heliospheric plasma, provide a mechanistic explanation of the radial evolution and eventual disappearance of the outward-propagating Alfvénic fluctuations. In particular, identify and quantify the roles played by:
(i) large-scale velocity shear between fast and slow solar wind streams (including shear-driven nonlinear couplings and Kelvin–Helmholtz-type instabilities beyond the Alfvén critical zone);
(ii) stream–stream interaction regions where fast wind overtakes slow wind, producing compressions and rarefactions that stir the fluctuations;
(iii) proximity to the heliospheric current sheet, where the radial mean magnetic field weakens and transverse gradients in the background field become important.

Explain how these three mechanisms together drive the cascade and decorrelation that transform a population of outward-propagating Alfvén waves emitted from the corona into the observed turbulent state of the outer heliosphere.

Let $d\ge 2$ be an integer. The Erdős–Rényi $d$-uniform random hypergraph $G_d(N,1/2)$ has vertex set $[N]=\{1,\dots,N\}$ and each of the $\binom{N}{d}$ possible $d$-element hyperedges is included independently with probability $1/2$. The planted model $G_d(N,1/2,\kappa)$ is obtained by first sampling $G_d(N,1/2)$, then choosing a uniformly random subset $K\subseteq[N]$ with $|K|=\kappa$ and adding every $d$-element subset of $K$ as a hyperedge (so $K$ becomes a hypergraphic clique).

The hypergraphic planted clique (HPC) detection problem asks, given a single sample, to distinguish
$$H_0:\ G\sim G_d(N,1/2)\quad\text{versus}\quad H_1:\ G\sim G_d(N,1/2,\kappa).$$
A (possibly randomized) test $\psi:\{\text{hypergraphs on }[N]\}\to\{0,1\}$ is said to solve detection asymptotically if
$$\mathbb{P}_{H_0}[\psi=1]+\mathbb{P}_{H_1}[\psi=0]\longrightarrow 0\quad\text{as }N\to\infty.$$
It is conjectured that, for any fixed $d\ge 3$ and any $\kappa=\kappa(N)$ with $\kappa\le N^{1/2-\varepsilon}$ for some $\varepsilon>0$, no polynomial-time test solves HPC detection.

Problem. Provide rigorous evidence for this conjectured computational hardness within additional restricted models of computation beyond Metropolis-type algorithms and low-degree polynomial tests. Specifically, prove unconditional lower bounds (matching the threshold $\kappa\asymp\sqrt{N}$) for the HPC detection problem under at least one of the following algorithmic frameworks:
(a) statistical query algorithms (with an appropriate query and tolerance model for hypergraph distributions);
(b) the sum-of-squares hierarchy, by exhibiting pseudo-expectations of degree growing with $N$ that certify indistinguishability when $\kappa=o(\sqrt{N})$;
(c) belief propagation on the associated factor graph;
(d) approximate message passing algorithms;
or any other natural restricted computational model.

Determine whether the hadronisation of heavy quarks (charm and beauty) is a universal process across different collision systems (e+e−, ep, pp, p–A, and A–A), or whether it instead exhibits system-dependent features that require collision-system-specific mechanisms such as: (i) hadronisation via quark coalescence/recombination in a dense partonic medium; (ii) the contribution of so-far-unobserved higher-mass excited charm-baryon states feeding down to ground-state baryons; or (iii) string formation beyond the leading-colour approximation (e.g. colour-reconnection or junction mechanisms).

Concretely, identify a set of observables and a quantitative framework that discriminate between these competing hadronisation pictures. The observables of interest include the production cross sections, transverse-momentum spectra, baryon-to-meson ratios, and fragmentation fractions of the following heavy-flavour hadrons measured across collision systems:
 • single-charm and single-beauty baryons: Λc+, Λb0, Ξc+, Ξc0, Ωc0;
 • the doubly-heavy meson Bc+ (containing b and c quarks);
 • multi-charm baryons: Ξcc+, Ξcc++, Ωcc+, Ωccc++;
 • exotic heavy-flavour hadrons such as X(3872) and Tcc+.

The problem is to:
(a) formulate criteria, in terms of these observables, under which the hypothesis of universal heavy-quark hadronisation is falsified;
(b) determine which combinations of measurements among the hadrons listed above are sufficient to distinguish between coalescence-based models, models invoking an augmented spectrum of higher-mass charm baryons, and models with non-leading-colour string formation; and
(c) characterise how the production of exotic states such as X(3872) and Tcc+ depends on the hadronisation mechanism and the collision environment.

Consider a reinforcement-learning agent interacting with an unknown, possibly stochastic environment modeled as a Markov decision process $(\mathcal{S},\mathcal{A},P,r,\gamma)$, where the transition kernel $P$ and reward function $r$ are unknown, and the state space $\mathcal{S}$ is endowed with an unknown safety specification (e.g., a set of constraint functions $g_i:\mathcal{S}\times\mathcal{A}\to\mathbb{R}$ such that trajectories must satisfy $g_i(s_t,a_t)\le 0$ for all $t$, almost surely or with high probability). Assume only that some general physical principles (such as conservation laws, symmetries, dissipativity, or Lipschitz/passivity properties) are known to govern the dynamics, but no approximated parametric or analytic system model is given, and no a priori specified "safe set" of states is provided.

Devise a reinforcement-learning algorithm and accompanying theory that:

1. Uses physics-based inductive biases (rather than a specific learned system model or a pre-given safe-state set) to enforce the safety constraints with high probability during both exploration and the deployment of the learned policy;
2. Achieves a provable (or empirically validated) regret or sample-complexity bound for converging to an optimal safe policy;
3. Generalizes across different tasks and environments sharing the same class of physical principles, without re-engineering domain-specific safe sets or system identification procedures.

Formally characterize the assumptions on the physics priors that are necessary and sufficient for such a model-agnostic safe exploration and control scheme to exist, and construct an explicit algorithm realizing these guarantees.

Consider the setting of reinforcement learning (RL), in which an agent interacts with an environment modeled as a Markov decision process (S, A, P, R, γ) consisting of a state space S, action space A, transition kernel P(s'|s,a), reward function R(s,a), and discount factor γ ∈ [0,1), with the goal of learning a policy π: S → A that maximizes the expected discounted return E[Σ_t γ^t R(s_t, a_t)]. A "physics prior" is any source of structured prior knowledge about the environment that arises from physical laws — for example, ordinary or partial differential equations governing dynamics, conservation laws (energy, momentum, mass), symmetries and invariances, dimensional analysis constraints, Lagrangian/Hamiltonian structure, known equilibrium or stability conditions, or approximate analytical/simulated models. A physics-informed reinforcement learning (PIRL) algorithm is one that integrates one or more such physics priors into one or more components of the RL pipeline (state/action representation, environment/dynamics model, reward shaping, policy or value-function class, exploration strategy, loss regularization, or safety constraints).

Problem. Construct and rigorously specify a comprehensive selection-and-organization framework F that, given as input a description of a novel physical task T (including available physical knowledge about T and the RL components it admits), outputs a principled choice and arrangement of physics priors to be embedded into the RL algorithm for T. The framework F must satisfy:

(i) Generality: F applies across physical domains and is not tailored to a single task;
(ii) Variability handling: F accommodates the empirical fact that the most effective physics priors may differ between tasks even within the same physical domain;
(iii) Component placement: F prescribes at which stage(s) of the RL pipeline each selected prior is integrated;
(iv) Reusability: F reduces the need to engineer each PIRL task from scratch, providing a systematic procedure rather than ad hoc design;
(v) Justification: F comes with criteria (theoretical, statistical, or empirical) for why the prescribed choice of priors is appropriate for T.

Provide such a framework, together with a precise specification of its inputs, outputs, decision procedure, and the criteria used to validate its prescriptions on novel physical tasks.

Consider the Einstein–Vlasov–Maxwell system on a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold (M, g), describing a collisionless ensemble of identical particles of rest mass m > 0 and electric charge q ∈ ℝ that interact through both gravity and the electromagnetic field. The unknowns are:

(i) a Lorentzian metric g of signature (−,+,+,+) on M;
(ii) an electromagnetic 2-form F on M with sourced Maxwell equations dF = 0 and ∇^μ F_{μν} = 4π J_ν;
(iii) a nonnegative distribution function f on the mass shell P_m = {(x,p) ∈ TM : g_x(p,p) = −m², p future-directed},

coupled through:
• the Einstein equations R_{μν} − ½ R g_{μν} = 8π (T^{V}_{μν} + T^{EM}_{μν}), where T^{V}_{μν}(x) = ∫_{P_m(x)} f(x,p) p_μ p_ν dvol_{P_m(x)}(p) and T^{EM}_{μν} = (1/4π)(F_{μα} F_ν^{\;α} − ¼ g_{μν} F_{αβ} F^{αβ});
• the charged-particle Vlasov equation along the Lorentz–geodesic flow: p^μ ∂_{x^μ} f + (−Γ^i_{μν} p^μ p^ν + q F^i_{\;μ} p^μ) ∂_{p^i} f = 0;
• the current J^μ(x) = q ∫_{P_m(x)} f(x,p) p^μ dvol_{P_m(x)}(p).

Assume the spacetime admits two commuting Killing vector fields, a timelike ξ and an axial η, generating a stationary and axially symmetric isometry group, with the standard asymptotic flatness conditions at spatial infinity, and that F is invariant under both Killing fields.

Problem. Develop a framework (analytic, numerical, or a combination) that produces stationary, axially symmetric, asymptotically flat solutions (g, F, f) of the above Einstein–Vlasov–Maxwell system with f compactly supported on the mass shell, without restricting to a perturbative regime: that is, allow the total ADM angular momentum J and a measure of gravitational-field strength (e.g., the central redshift or 2M/R ratio) to range over an unrestricted regime, rather than being constrained to a small neighborhood of a known background (such as Minkowski space or a spherically symmetric solution). Establish existence of such solutions and describe their parameter space, including the admissible range of total charge Q, total angular momentum J, and gravitational compactness.

Consider a spatial single-cell dataset in which, for each cell $i$ in a population $\{1, \dots, N\}$, one observes (a) a gene expression vector $g_i \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{G}$ giving counts of $G$ transcript species, and (b) a high-resolution image $I_i$ (or segmented mask) capturing the cell's morphology. Gene expression admits a canonical tabular encoding as the count matrix $X \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}^{N \times G}$, but morphology has no analogous universal tabular representation. Two broad classes of morphology encodings are available:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Engineered feature maps $\phi_{\mathrm{eng}}: I_i \mapsto m_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ producing interpretable handcrafted descriptors (e.g. area, perimeter, eccentricity, intensity moments, texture features).
\item Learned embeddings $\phi_{\theta}: I_i \mapsto z_i \in \mathbb{R}^{k}$ obtained from a neural network trained with a self-supervised, supervised, or multimodal objective.
\end{enumerate}
Determine a representation $\phi$ of cellular morphology that is optimal for joint statistical analysis with the gene expression count matrix $X$. Specifically:
\begin{enumerate}
\item Formulate criteria (e.g. preservation of biologically meaningful variation, alignment with transcriptomic structure, invariance to nuisance imaging factors, identifiability, interpretability) under which optimality of $\phi$ can be defined.
\item Decide whether a fixed engineered $\phi_{\mathrm{eng}}$, a learned $\phi_{\theta}$, or a hybrid construction best satisfies these criteria.
\item Provide a construction of $\phi$ together with a joint model $p(g_i, \phi(I_i))$ enabling integrated downstream tasks such as cell-type identification, spatial domain inference, and morphology--expression association testing.
\end{enumerate}

Let $\Gamma$ be a $(d-1)$-dimensional simplicial complex on vertex set $\{1,\dots,n\}$, and let $I_\Gamma \subset S=\mathbb{K}[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ (with $\mathbb{K}$ an infinite field of characteristic $0$) be its Stanley–Reisner ideal. Let $\Theta=(\theta_1,\dots,\theta_d)$ be a generic linear system of parameters for $S/I_\Gamma$ and let $w$ be a generic linear form. One says that the Artinian reduction $A(\Gamma):=S/(I_\Gamma+(\Theta))$ has the almost strong Lefschetz property if, for every $k\le (d-1)/2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] the multiplication map $\cdot w^{\,d-2k-1}: A(\Gamma)_k \to A(\Gamma)_{d-1-k}$ is injective;
\item[(ii)] the multiplication map $\cdot w^{\,d-2k-1}: A(\Gamma)_{k+1} \to A(\Gamma)_{d-k}$ is surjective.
\end{itemize}
It is known that barycentric subdivisions of shellable (more generally, suitable) simplicial complexes satisfy properties (i) and (ii).

Problem: Exhibit, or classify, further classes of simplicial complexes $\Gamma$ (different from barycentric subdivisions) whose Stanley–Reisner Artinian reductions $S/(I_\Gamma+(\Theta))$ satisfy the almost strong Lefschetz conditions (i) and (ii) above.

Background. In standard cosmology, the observed anisotropies of the cosmic microwave background (CMB) are well described by a nearly scale-invariant primordial power spectrum P(k) of curvature fluctuations, conventionally derived from quantum field theory on a curved (typically quasi-de Sitter) spacetime sourced by a hypothetical scalar "inflaton" field. While phenomenologically successful, inflaton-based models are introduced as effective fields and have not been derived from, nor naturally embedded in, a more fundamental theory of quantum gravity.

Problem. Construct a fundamental, quantum-gravity-compatible model — i.e., a model whose underlying degrees of freedom are those of a candidate theory of quantum gravity (for example a discrete or non-perturbative theory of spacetime such as a causal-set, spin-foam, loop, or analogous framework) and which does not postulate an ad hoc inflaton scalar field — that:

(1) predicts, from first principles, a primordial spectrum of quantum fluctuations of geometric/matter degrees of freedom on cosmological scales whose two-point function reproduces the observed nearly scale-invariant CMB temperature and polarization power spectra, including the measured amplitude, spectral tilt, and constraints on running and non-Gaussianity;

(2) provides a precise dynamical mechanism by which these primordial quantum fluctuations decohere/classicalize and seed the growth of large-scale structure consistent with present observations of galaxy clustering and the matter power spectrum;

(3) reduces, in an appropriate semiclassical/continuum limit, to general relativity coupled to the observed matter content, so that the standard ΛCDM evolution after recombination is recovered.

Specify the model, derive the predicted primordial power spectrum and higher-order correlators, and exhibit the structure-formation mechanism, demonstrating compatibility with current cosmological data without invoking a fundamental inflaton field.

Background and setup. A causal set is a locally finite partially ordered set $(C,\preceq)$, intended as a discrete model of Lorentzian spacetime in which $x\preceq y$ encodes causal precedence. For a scalar field $\phi:C\to\mathbb{R}$ on a causal set sprinkled (via a Poisson process of unit density) into $D$-dimensional Minkowski spacetime, one can define a family of retarded, Lorentz-invariant, nonlocal linear operators
$$
(B^{(D)}\phi)(x) \;=\; \frac{1}{\ell^{2}}\!\left(\,a\,\phi(x) \;+\; \sum_{k}\,b_{k}\sum_{y\in L_{k}(x)}\phi(y)\right),
$$
where $\ell$ is a nonlocality length scale, $L_{k}(x)$ denotes the $k$-th causal "layer" of points to the past of $x$ (defined via the order interval cardinalities $|I(y,x)|=k-1$), and the constants $a,b_{k}$ are chosen so that, after averaging over sprinklings, the operator converges in the continuum limit to the standard d'Alembertian $\Box$ acting on smooth scalar fields. These nonlocal operators $B^{(D)}$ provide a causal-set analogue of $\Box$ and underlie a class of "off-shell" propagation models proposed as candidates for dark matter phenomenology.

Problem. Construct (or prove the existence/non-existence of) a generalization of the nonlocal causal-set d'Alembertian framework above to non-scalar fields. Specifically, formulate retarded, Lorentz-invariant, nonlocal linear differential-type operators on causal sets that act on:

(a) vector fields (sections of a bundle over the causal set that limits, in the continuum, to the tangent or cotangent bundle of Minkowski spacetime), reproducing in the continuum the appropriate wave operator on $1$-forms or vectors;

(b) spinor fields, reproducing in the continuum the Dirac operator (or its square) on spinor fields in $D$-dimensional Minkowski spacetime;

(c) tensor fields of arbitrary rank.

In each case, the construction must:
(i) be defined intrinsically on the causal set, using only the order relation and discrete combinatorial data of the sprinkling;
(ii) be retarded (depending only on the causal past of each point);
(iii) be Lorentz invariant in expectation under the Poisson sprinkling;
(iv) converge, in mean, to the corresponding continuum wave operator as the discreteness scale tends to zero with $\ell$ held fixed (or in an appropriate continuum limit).

Determine whether such a construction is possible, and if so, exhibit it explicitly.

Let $M$ be a closed manifold of even dimension $2m$, and let $\chi(M)$ denote its Euler characteristic. Recall that $M$ is said to be aspherical if its universal cover is contractible (equivalently, $\pi_k(M)=0$ for all $k\geq 2$). For a closed smooth Riemannian manifold, $\sec(M)$ denotes its sectional curvature function on 2-planes in tangent spaces, and inequalities such as $\sec(M)<0$, $\sec(M)\le 0$, $\sec(M)=0$, $\sec(M)\ge 0$, $\sec(M)>0$ are understood pointwise for all tangent 2-planes.

Prove or disprove the following statements:

(a) (Topological Hopf conjecture.) If $M$ is a closed aspherical topological manifold of even dimension $2m$, then
$$(-1)^m\,\chi(M)\;\ge\;0.$$

(b) (Riemannian Hopf conjectures.) If $M$ is a closed smooth manifold of even dimension $2m$ equipped with a Riemannian metric, then:
   (i) $(-1)^m\,\chi(M) > 0$ whenever $\sec(M)<0$;
   (ii) $(-1)^m\,\chi(M) \ge 0$ whenever $\sec(M)\le 0$;
   (iii) $\chi(M) = 0$ whenever $\sec(M)=0$;
   (iv) $\chi(M) \ge 0$ whenever $\sec(M)\ge 0$;
   (v) $\chi(M) > 0$ whenever $\sec(M)>0$.

Consider the quadratic complex Hénon map $H_2 : \mathbb{C}^2 \to \mathbb{C}^2$ defined by
$$H_2(x, y) = (y,\, x + y^2).$$
The point $(0,0) \in \mathbb{C}^2$ is a fixed point of $H_2$. The differential $DH_2(0,0)$ has eigenvalues equal to the two roots of $\lambda^2 = \lambda \cdot 0 + 1$, namely the golden-ratio-type values $\lambda_{\pm} = \tfrac{1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}$; in particular one eigenvalue is hyperbolic expanding and the other is hyperbolic of absolute value less than $1$, but the fixed point is not linearizable in the usual sense over $\mathbb{C}$ because of resonances/parabolic-like obstructions arising from the algebraic relations among the eigenvalues, and the global polynomial nature of $H_2$ leads to subtle behavior of stable and unstable manifolds in a full neighborhood of the origin.

Problem: Give a complete description of the local dynamics of $H_2$ in a neighborhood of the origin in $\mathbb{C}^2$. In particular, describe:
\begin{itemize}
\item the structure of the local stable and unstable manifolds $W^s_{\mathrm{loc}}(0)$ and $W^u_{\mathrm{loc}}(0)$ and their intersection behavior;
\item whether there exist orbits of $H_2$ that remain in an arbitrarily small neighborhood of the origin for all positive and negative iterates (i.e., points in $\bigcap_{n\in\mathbb{Z}} H_2^n(U)$ for arbitrarily small open neighborhoods $U$ of the origin) other than $0$ itself, and if so what is their structure;
\item the existence or non-existence of small periodic cycles, homoclinic/heteroclinic tangencies, and invariant analytic/Fatou objects accumulating at the origin;
\item a normal form or conjugacy class describing $H_2$ restricted to a neighborhood of $0$ in $\mathbb{C}^2$.
\end{itemize}

Let $K$ be a number field, fix integers $B\geq 1$ and $d\geq 2$. A generalized Hénon map of degree $d$ over $K$ is an automorphism $f:\mathbb{A}^2\to\mathbb{A}^2$ of the form $f=f_1\circ f_2\circ\cdots\circ f_m$, where each $f_i(x,y)=(y,\,p_i(y)-a_i x)$ with $p_i\in K[y]$ of degree $d_i\geq 2$, $a_i\in K^\times$, and $d=d_1d_2\cdots d_m$. Associated to such an $f$ are:
\begin{itemize}
\item the canonical height $\hat h_f:\mathbb{A}^2(\overline K)\to[0,\infty)$, characterized (up to standard normalizations) by $\hat h_f = \hat h_f^+ + \hat h_f^-$ where $\hat h_f^\pm(P)=\lim_{n\to\infty}d^{-n}h^+(f^{\pm n}P)$ for a suitable Weil-type height $h^+$ on $\mathbb{A}^2$;
\item the height $h(f)$ of $f$, defined as the Weil height of the tuple of coefficients of $f$ (viewed as a point in a suitable projective space of coefficients).
\end{itemize}

Question: Do there exist constants $\varepsilon>0$ and $C\geq 0$, depending only on $K$, $B$, and $d$, such that for every generalized Hénon map $f$ of degree $d$ defined over $K$, the set
$$\bigl\{P\in\mathbb{A}^2(\overline K)\;:\;[K(P):K]\leq B \text{ and }\hat h_f(P)<\varepsilon\, h(f)+C\bigr\}$$
has cardinality bounded above by a constant depending only on $K$, $B$, and $d$ (and not on $f$)?

Work in the complex projective plane $\mathbb{P}^2 = \mathbb{P}^2_{\mathbb{C}}$ with homogeneous coordinate ring $S = \mathbb{C}[x_0,x_1,x_2]$. Let $C : f = 0$ be a reduced plane curve of degree $d$ with only isolated singularities, where $f \in S_d$. Let $J_f = (\partial_0 f, \partial_1 f, \partial_2 f) \subset S$ be the Jacobian ideal and let $N(f) = I_f / J_f$, where $I_f$ is the saturation of $J_f$ with respect to the maximal ideal $(x_0,x_1,x_2)$. The graded module $N(f) = \bigoplus_j N(f)_j$ is finite dimensional, and one defines the freeness defect
$$\nu(C) \;=\; \max_{j} \dim_{\mathbb{C}} N(f)_j.$$
A reduced curve $C$ is called free if and only if $\nu(C) = 0$.

Question: Is the invariant $\nu(C)$ determined by the following two pieces of data alone:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the degree $d$ of $C$;
\item[(ii)] the unordered list of analytic isomorphism types of the isolated singularities of $C$ (equivalently, for each singular point $p \in \mathrm{Sing}(C)$, the analytic isomorphism class of the local singularity $(C,p)$)?
\end{enumerate}
That is, do any two reduced plane curves in $\mathbb{P}^2$ with isolated singularities sharing the same degree and the same multiset of analytic singularity types necessarily have the same value of $\nu$?

Consider the class of soft amorphous materials: disordered viscoelastic solids — including emulsions, polymer and colloidal gels, microgels, foams, concentrated particulate suspensions, and similar yield-stress fluids — that under sufficient mechanical forcing undergo a yielding transition from a solid-like response to a liquid-like flowing state. Empirically, this transition may appear "ductile" (a gradual, spatially homogeneous fluidization with smooth stress evolution) or "brittle" (an abrupt macroscopic stress drop, often accompanied by fracture-like or shear-banded localization).

Provide a predictive characterization of the ductile-to-brittle transition in such materials. Specifically, identify and quantify how the following control variables determine whether yielding is brittle or ductile, and at what threshold the crossover between the two regimes occurs:

(a) intrinsic material properties (elastic moduli, interaction potentials, attraction strength, polydispersity, particle/network architecture);
(b) preparation history and aging (quench protocol, age, prior shear and memory imprinting);
(c) confining geometry and boundary conditions (gap size, wall roughness, slip, free surfaces);
(d) deformation protocol (imposed strain rate, stress ramp rate, oscillatory amplitude and frequency, creep);
(e) microscopic-to-mesoscopic processes (plastic rearrangements, shear transformation zones, elastic interactions, damage accumulation, cavitation, strain localization).

Develop a framework — connecting microscopic dynamics to macroscopic constitutive response across the relevant length and time scales — that predicts, for a given soft amorphous system and protocol, whether yielding will be brittle or ductile, and that identifies the order parameter(s) and control parameter(s) governing the transition.

Consider amorphous solids subjected to mechanical deformation. Two broad classes are of interest:

(i) "Hard glasses" (e.g., metallic, oxide, or molecular glasses), in which the constituents are athermal on the experimental timescale and yielding is commonly analyzed in the athermal quasi-static (AQS) limit, where the strain rate is taken to zero and inertial/thermal effects are negligible. In this setting, the yielding transition can switch from a smooth, ductile crossover (gradual stress overshoot followed by a flowing plateau) to a sharp, brittle response (a discontinuous macroscopic stress drop) as a function of preparation history (e.g., degree of annealing).

(ii) "Soft glassy materials" (e.g., dense emulsions, foams, colloidal gels and glasses, microgel pastes), which generically exhibit dual solid–liquid viscoelastic behavior at rest and a stress response that depends strongly on the imposed strain rate, requiring finite-rate descriptions of yielding rather than purely quasi-static ones.

Formulate a single conceptual framework — for instance, a constitutive or mesoscopic plasticity model with a tunable "brittleness" parameter, together with control parameters encoding preparation/age and the dimensionless ratio of the imposed strain rate to an internal relaxation rate — that:

(a) reproduces the ductile-to-brittle transition observed in hard glasses in the athermal quasi-static limit;

(b) reproduces the ductile-to-brittle transition observed in soft amorphous materials at finite strain rates, while incorporating their viscoelastic (solid–liquid) character;

(c) recovers the AQS hard-glass behavior as a definite limit of the soft-glass description (e.g., as relaxation time over loading time tends to infinity), and conversely accounts for finite-rate corrections to AQS yielding;

(d) predicts, from a small set of microscopic or mesoscopic ingredients, on which side of the ductile-to-brittle transition a given material lies as a function of preparation, strain rate, and viscoelastic relaxation spectrum.

Determine whether such a unified framework exists, and if so identify its minimal ingredients; otherwise establish a fundamental obstruction to merging the two descriptions.

Consider the standard families of computational models used to describe the mechanics of epithelia and multicellular tissues:

(a) Cellular Potts models, in which cells are represented by domains on a lattice and dynamics are governed by Monte Carlo updates minimizing an energy functional combining adhesion, area, and perimeter terms;
(b) Self-propelled Voronoi models, in which each cell is identified with the Voronoi region of a point in the plane and the energy of a configuration is a sum over cells of the form K_A (A_i - A_0)^2 + K_P (P_i - P_0)^2, with A_i and P_i the area and perimeter of cell i;
(c) Vertex models, in which the tissue is represented as a polygonal tiling whose vertices evolve under forces derived from a similar area/perimeter energy together with line tensions on edges.

Develop an extension of these models that simultaneously and self-consistently incorporates the following physical ingredients, and analyze the resulting rheological behavior (in particular the existence and value of characteristic time scales, critical shear rates separating flow regimes, and the size of cooperative rearrangement domains):

1. An intrinsic relaxation time scale τ and a critical shear rate γ̇_c above which the tissue response changes qualitatively;
2. A well-defined effective viscosity, addressing the difficulty that in deforming epithelia an unambiguous viscosity may not exist;
3. Spatially extended cooperative rearrangement domains rather than purely local T1 events;
4. A controlled relaxation of the two-dimensional monolayer assumption, allowing out-of-plane deformations and three-dimensional cell shapes;
5. Boundary dissipation mechanisms distinguishing cell–substrate friction from cell–cell pair dissipation;
6. Cell–substrate adhesion and the rigidity of an underlying extracellular matrix, including the case of a deformable substrate;
7. Geometries appropriate to organoids and tumor spheroids (closed surfaces, curved interfaces, finite three-dimensional aggregates);
8. Molecular-scale mechanochemical sensitivity, in which local mechanical stresses feed back on cellular parameters (line tensions, target areas/perimeters, motility) through internal biochemical variables.

Specify the equations of motion, identify the dimensionless parameters that emerge, and determine the conditions under which such an extended model exhibits a ductile-to-brittle transition in its yielding response.

Consider a wireless radio-access network deployment intended to realize user-centric cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO). The deployment consists of a large set of geographically distributed access points (APs), each equipped with one or more antennas and connected via fronthaul links to processing units. Users are served without fixed cell boundaries: for each user equipment (UE), a dynamically chosen subset of APs (the user's serving cluster) coherently transmits in the downlink and jointly processes in the uplink, with cluster membership changing over time as users move and as load conditions vary.

Assume the network is organized following an Open Radio Access Network (O-RAN) style split, comprising:
(i) Open Radio Units (O-RUs) co-located with APs handling lower-physical-layer functions;
(ii) Open Distributed Units (O-DUs), each managing a fixed geographic subset of O-RUs and performing higher physical-layer and MAC functions;
(iii) Open Centralized Units (O-CUs) implementing higher-layer functions;
(iv) a RAN Intelligent Controller (RIC) for orchestration.

A user-centric cluster for a UE may contain APs whose serving O-RUs are connected to different O-DUs, so coherent joint transmission/reception requires exchanging channel estimates, precoded data, and scheduling information across O-DU boundaries. The current O-RAN specifications do not define an inter-O-DU coordination interface.

Problem: Specify a practical, scalable RAN architecture for user-centric CF-MMIMO that

(a) defines the functional split between O-RU, O-DU, O-CU, and RIC compatible with O-RAN principles;

(b) defines an inter-O-DU coordination interface (signaling, data exchanged, timing, and synchronization requirements) sufficient to support coherent joint transmission/reception across O-DU boundaries;

(c) supports formation, maintenance, and handover of user-centric AP clusters that change dynamically with user load and locations, with computational complexity per AP, per O-DU, and per UE, and fronthaul traffic per link, all scaling at most polylogarithmically (or otherwise sublinearly) in the total number of APs and UEs as the network grows;

(d) is realizable with finite-capacity fronthaul links and bounded processing latency consistent with 5G/6G timing budgets.

Provide such an architecture together with a proof or quantitative argument establishing its scalability and feasibility, or prove that no architecture satisfying (a)–(d) simultaneously exists.

Consider a cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO) wireless network consisting of L access points (APs), each equipped with N antennas and connected through a fronthaul to one or more central processing units (CPUs), jointly serving K single-antenna users on the same time–frequency resources. Let h_{l,k} ∈ ℂ^N denote the (block-fading) channel between AP l and user k, and let p^{ul}_k ≥ 0 and η_{l,k} ≥ 0 denote the uplink transmit power of user k and the downlink power coefficient used by AP l for user k, subject to per-user uplink power constraints p^{ul}_k ≤ P^{ul}_{max} and per-AP downlink power constraints ∑_k η_{l,k} E{‖w_{l,k}‖²} ≤ P^{dl}_{max}, where w_{l,k} are precoding/combining vectors. Define an implementation of uplink and downlink power control as scalable if the computational complexity per AP and per user, the amount of CSI sharing across the fronthaul, and the signaling overhead remain bounded as L, K → ∞.

Develop a framework of uplink and downlink power-control schemes for CF-MMIMO that are scalable in the above sense and that simultaneously address:
(i) sum spectral efficiency maximization;
(ii) user fairness, e.g. max–min or proportional-fairness criteria on the per-user achievable rates log_2(1 + SINR_k);
(iii) mitigation of the near-far effect arising from heterogeneous large-scale fading;
(iv) inter-user interference control;
(v) the trade-off between fully centralized optimization at a CPU and fully distributed computation at individual APs/users;
(vi) operation under only partial CSI sharing between APs and CPUs;
(vii) the design and provable performance limits of machine-learning and reinforcement-learning-based power-control policies in this setting.

Specifically, determine to what extent any scalable power-control rule (heuristic and fully distributed, or sub-optimal and centralized with only partial CSI sharing) can approach the performance of the corresponding network-wide optimal but unscalable power allocation, and characterize the fundamental trade-offs among the criteria (i)–(vii).

Consider an ultra-dense cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO) wireless network consisting of a large number L of geographically distributed access points (APs), each equipped with N antennas, jointly serving K single-antenna user equipments (UEs) over the same time-frequency resources. The APs are connected to one or more central processing units via fronthaul links of limited capacity. Let H_l ∈ C^{N×K} denote the (estimated) channel matrix between AP l and the K UEs, and let v_{l,k} ∈ C^N denote the precoding/combining vector used at AP l for UE k.

For benchmark "network-wide" linear processing, one constructs precoders/combiners from the full aggregate channel matrix H = [H_1^T, …, H_L^T]^T ∈ C^{LN×K} using zero-forcing (ZF), V = H(H^H H)^{-1}, or MMSE-style filters; this requires global channel state information and the computation of a pseudo-inverse of size scaling with LN and K, plus high fronthaul overhead.

Because of the distributed topology, the interference power that a given UE receives from different APs (and vice versa) has a large dynamic range: only a small subset of intra-AP interference terms (between co-served UEs at the same AP) and inter-AP interference terms (between different APs) is significant.

Design a family of distributed precoding and combining schemes {v_{l,k}} for CF-MMIMO that simultaneously satisfy:
(i) each AP l only cancels a chosen small subset S_l^{intra} of intra-AP interferers and a small subset S_l^{inter} of inter-AP interferers, identified from large-scale fading and/or instantaneous channel statistics;
(ii) the only pseudo-inverse computations involved at any AP have size bounded by a constant independent of L and much smaller than min(LN, K);
(iii) the additional fronthaul signaling required to construct {v_{l,k}} is bounded independently of L (ideally zero beyond what fully local schemes need);
(iv) as the subsets S_l^{intra}, S_l^{inter} are enlarged, the per-user spectral efficiency provably converges to that of network-wide ZF (or MMSE) processing.

Provide such a scheme together with a rigorous performance analysis (closed-form or tight bounds on the achievable per-user signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio and spectral efficiency) demonstrating the claimed convergence and complexity/fronthaul scaling, or alternatively prove an impossibility/trade-off result quantifying the minimum complexity and fronthaul required to approach network-wide ZF/MMSE performance within a prescribed gap.

Consider an ultra-dense wireless network modeled as follows. A large set of single-antenna (or few-antenna) access points (APs) is distributed in a service area and connected via a fronthaul network to one or more central processing units. A set of user equipments (UEs) is served jointly by the APs in a coherent, cell-free massive MIMO (CF-MMIMO) regime: for each UE k, a user-centric cluster C_k of nearby APs cooperatively transmits/receives signals so that the effective channel seen by k is the superposition of the channels from all APs in C_k. The system requires:

(i) accurate channel state information (CSI) at each AP in C_k, typically acquired via uplink pilots within a coherence block of length τ_c symbols and coherence bandwidth B_c;
(ii) phase/frequency synchronization and reciprocity calibration across all APs in C_k so that coherent joint transmission is possible;
(iii) scheduling of pilot and data resources across UEs sharing pilots (pilot contamination control);
(iv) routing of baseband samples and CSI over a capacity-limited fronthaul of finite latency.

Now suppose UEs move with velocities v inducing Doppler spreads f_D = v f_c / c (with carrier frequency f_c) that are comparable to or larger than 1/τ_c per coherence block, so that the user-centric clusters {C_k}, the CSI estimates, the synchronization/calibration parameters, the scheduling decisions, and the fronthaul routing must all be updated on a timescale shorter than the channel coherence time.

Design and analyze a CF-MMIMO operating scheme — specifying cluster formation rules C_k(t), pilot allocation and CSI acquisition protocol, synchronization/calibration update rule, scheduler, and fronthaul routing policy — that achieves a provable lower bound on per-user spectral efficiency that does not degrade to zero as the user mobility (Doppler) increases up to a specified range, while keeping control-plane signaling, fronthaul load, and computational complexity scalable in the number of APs and UEs.

In particular, determine whether such a scheme exists and, if so, characterize the achievable trade-off between maximum tolerable user velocity, network density (APs per unit area), per-UE spectral efficiency, and signaling/fronthaul overhead.

Consider the Einstein–Yang–Mills (EYM) system on a 4-dimensional Lorentzian manifold (M,g) with an SU(2) connection A and curvature F = dA + A∧A. The equations are

  Ric(g) − (1/2) R(g) g = 8π T(F,g),   d_A ⋆ F = 0,

where T(F,g) is the Yang–Mills stress–energy tensor T_{μν} = (1/4π)( F_{μα} F_ν{}^α − (1/4) g_{μν} F_{αβ} F^{αβ} ).

Assume spherical symmetry: M admits an SO(3) action by isometries whose generic orbits are 2-spheres, and the metric takes the form

  g = −α(t,r)² dt² + a(t,r)² dr² + r² (dθ² + sin²θ dφ²),

with an SO(3)-invariant SU(2) Yang–Mills connection (parametrized in the standard way by a single real "magnetic" function w(t,r) and possibly an "electric" function, both depending on t and r).

Develop a rigorous mathematical analysis of the resulting time-dependent spherically symmetric EYM equations as a Cauchy problem (or characteristic initial value problem) for the unknowns (α, a, w, …), in analogy with the well-developed analysis of the spherically symmetric Einstein-scalar field system (which includes results on local and global well-posedness, formation of trapped surfaces, weak cosmic censorship, critical collapse, and the structure of singularities). In particular, establish results on:

(i) local existence, uniqueness, and continuation criteria for solutions arising from regular asymptotically flat initial data;
(ii) global behavior, including conditions for global regularity versus gravitational collapse and black hole formation;
(iii) the asymptotic structure (decay, completeness of null infinity) of global-in-time solutions;
(iv) weak and strong cosmic censorship within this symmetry class;
(v) critical phenomena in the threshold of black hole formation.

Let $k$ be a field and consider the standard graded polynomial ring $S_n = k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$. For a homogeneous ideal $I \subseteq S_n$, let $\operatorname{reg}(I)$ denote the Castelnuovo–Mumford regularity of $I$ (the smallest integer $r$ such that for every $i$, every minimal $i$-th syzygy of $I$ sits in degree at most $r+i$), and let $d(I)$ denote the largest degree appearing among a minimal homogeneous system of generators of $I$.

Fix a constant $D \geq 2$. Construct three explicit families of homogeneous ideals $\{I_n\}_{n\geq n_0}$, with $I_n \subseteq S_n$ and $d(I_n) \leq D$ for all $n$, such that:

(a) $\operatorname{reg}(I_n)$ grows polynomially in $n$ (i.e., $\operatorname{reg}(I_n) = \Theta(n^c)$ for some $c \geq 1$);

(b) $\operatorname{reg}(I_n)$ grows singly exponentially in $n$ (i.e., $\operatorname{reg}(I_n) = \Theta(a^n)$ for some $a>1$);

(c) $\operatorname{reg}(I_n)$ grows doubly exponentially in $n$ (i.e., $\operatorname{reg}(I_n) \geq 2^{2^{\,cn}}$ for some $c>0$ and sufficiently large $n$).

In each case the maximal degree $D$ of the generators must remain fixed independently of $n$.

Let $k$ be an infinite field and $S=k[x_1,\dots,x_n]$ the standard graded polynomial ring. Fix integers $c,n\ge 1$ and consider homogeneous ideals $I\subset S$ generated by a tuple $U=(f_1,\dots,f_c)$ of quadratic forms, and set $R=S/I$. Fix integers $p,q$ and a homogeneous presentation matrix $V$ of size $p\times q$ with linear entries over $R$, defining a finitely generated graded $R$-module $M=\mathrm{coker}(V)$. The data $(U,V)$ is parametrized by a (smooth, irreducible) affine variety $X$ whose points are pairs consisting of $c$ quadratic forms in $S$ together with a $p\times q$ matrix of linear forms.

Consider the following two loci in $X$:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(a)] $X_{\mathrm{const}} = \{(U,V)\in X : \text{the graded Betti numbers } \beta_{i,j}^{R}(\mathrm{coker}(V)) \text{ take a value independent of }(U,V) \text{ on }X_{\mathrm{const}}\}$;
\item[(b)] $X_{\mathrm{const,np}} = \{(U,V)\in X_{\mathrm{const}} : \text{the minimal graded free resolution of } \mathrm{coker}(V) \text{ over } R \text{ is not eventually periodic}\}$.
\end{itemize}

Question: 
\begin{enumerate}
\item Does $X_{\mathrm{const}}$ contain a nonempty Zariski open subset of $X$?
\item Does $X_{\mathrm{const,np}}$ contain a nonempty Zariski open subset of $X$?
\end{enumerate}

Let a cluster graph of cluster depth 2 be a pair C = (G, T) where G = (V, E) is an undirected graph and T is a rooted tree of depth 2 whose leaves are exactly the vertices V of G, so that the internal (non-root) nodes of T define a partition of V into clusters. Suppose we are given two such cluster graphs C1 = (G1, T1) and C2 = (G2, T2) (modeling two biological networks that describe different functional aspects of the same underlying system), each equipped with a prescribed "conventional" planar layout π1 and π2 (a fixed drawing or mental map). Suppose further we are given a mapping Φ between the vertices and clusters of C1 and those of C2, which may be 1:1, 1:n, or n:m, and let F denote the set of edges induced by Φ between V(G1) and V(G2). Form the union graph G = G1 ∪ G2 ∪ (V(G1) ∪ V(G2), F).

Devise an algorithm that produces a layout π(G) of G satisfying the following requirements simultaneously:
(i) the layout reveals interactions between the two networks via the mapping edges F;
(ii) the layout displays the mapping Φ legibly, including its 1:n and n:m cases;
(iii) the cluster structures given by T1 and T2 are respected (vertices of the same cluster are drawn contiguously and clusters are visually identifiable);
(iv) the conventional layouts π1 of G1 and π2 of G2 are respected as subdrawings of π(G);
(v) disconnected components of one network are integrated into the layout of the other when this is appropriate given Φ;
(vi) the algorithm provides a tunable trade-off between preservation of the given layout/mental map of G1 and achieving an improved (e.g., aesthetically or readability-optimized) representation of G2.

Provide a formal specification, an algorithm realizing it, and quality measures under which the resulting layout can be evaluated.

Background and definitions. An effect algebra is a partial algebra (E, ⊕, 0, 1) where ⊕ is a partial binary operation that is commutative and associative (in the strong sense), such that for every a ∈ E there is a unique a' ∈ E with a ⊕ a' = 1, and such that a ⊕ 1 being defined forces a = 0. A partial order is given by a ≤ b iff there exists c with a ⊕ c = b. An element w ∈ E is sharp if w ∧ w' = 0. The effect algebra E is called:
 • lattice effect algebra, if (E, ≤) is a lattice;
 • Archimedean, if for every nonzero a ∈ E there is a positive integer n with na defined but (n+1)a undefined (where na = a ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ a, n times);
 • atomic, if for every nonzero x ∈ E there exists an atom a (a minimal nonzero element) with a ≤ x;
 • sharply dominating, if for every a ∈ E there is a smallest sharp element â ≥ a;
 • homogeneous, if whenever u ≤ v₁ ⊕ v₂ with v₁, v₂ ≤ u', there exist u₁, u₂ ≤ u with u = u₁ ⊕ u₂ and uᵢ ≤ vᵢ.

For an atom a of E, its order is the largest positive integer n such that na is defined. By an orthogonal sum we mean an iterated ⊕.

Known fact. In every sharply dominating Archimedean atomic lattice effect algebra E, every nonzero x ∈ E admits a unique basic decomposition

  x = w_x ⊕ ⨁_{κ} k_κ a_κ,

where w_x is sharp, the a_κ are mutually orthogonal atoms, and each k_κ is a positive integer strictly less than the order of a_κ. (This is the "basic decomposition of elements" property, BDE.)

Question. Does every sharply dominating homogeneous Archimedean atomic effect algebra (without assuming the lattice property) admit an analogous basic decomposition of each of its elements by atoms?

Background. Let $\mathrm{MS}$ denote the variable-free modal language for metric spaces with the following syntax. Fix a countable set of propositional variables. Formulas are built from propositional variables using Boolean connectives and a family of unary modal operators $\exists^{<a}$ and $\exists^{\le a}$, for every positive rational $a$ (with duals $\forall^{<a}$, $\forall^{\le a}$). An $\mathrm{MS}$-model is a pair $\mathfrak{M}=(X,d,\nu)$ where $(X,d)$ is a metric space and $\nu$ assigns to each propositional variable a subset of $X$. The semantics is defined recursively for $x \in X$ by:
\begin{itemize}
\item $\mathfrak{M},x \models p$ iff $x \in \nu(p)$, with Boolean cases standard;
\item $\mathfrak{M},x \models \exists^{<a}\varphi$ iff there exists $y \in X$ with $d(x,y)<a$ and $\mathfrak{M},y \models \varphi$;
\item $\mathfrak{M},x \models \exists^{\le a}\varphi$ iff there exists $y \in X$ with $d(x,y)\le a$ and $\mathfrak{M},y \models \varphi$.
\end{itemize}
A formula $\varphi$ is satisfiable over a class $\mathcal{C}$ of metric spaces if there is a model whose underlying space lies in $\mathcal{C}$ and a point in it satisfying $\varphi$.

Problem. For natural fragments of $\mathrm{MS}$ (for example, fragments restricting allowed modal operators, allowed parameters, or modal depth), determine the decidability and computational complexity of the satisfiability problem when the class $\mathcal{C}$ consists of bounded subspaces of $n$-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^n$ (with the inherited Euclidean metric), including in particular the unit square $[0,1]\times[0,1] \subset \mathbb{R}^2$.

Setup. Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space. Consider the two-sorted first-order language $L$ with:
- a sort $D$ for positive real numbers, intended to be interpreted as distances, equipped with the usual ordering and the constants/relations needed to compare distances;
- a sort $P$ for points of $X$;
- a binary relation symbol $d(x,y)<a$ (and $d(x,y)\le a$, etc.) where $x,y$ are point variables and $a$ is a distance variable, interpreted in the obvious way in $(X,d)$;
- quantifiers over both sorts.

Let $L^2$ denote the fragment of $L$ in which only two variables of the point sort $P$ may be used (any number of distance variables of sort $D$ are allowed), with formulas having one free point variable.

Define the modal language MS over metric models as the propositional modal language whose formulas are built from propositional variables, Booleans, and unary "distance operators" $\langle{<a}\rangle\varphi$, $\langle{\le a}\rangle\varphi$, $\langle{>a}\rangle\varphi$, $\langle{\ge a}\rangle\varphi$ for each positive real (or rational) constant $a$, where $\langle{<a}\rangle\varphi$ holds at a point $x\in X$ iff there exists $y\in X$ with $d(x,y)<a$ such that $\varphi$ holds at $y$ (and similarly for the other operators).

Define QMS as the extension of MS in which the parameters $a$ inside distance operators are allowed to be variables ranging over positive reals (or positive rationals), and in which one may quantify $\forall a\,\varphi$ and $\exists a\,\varphi$ over those distance variables.

Question. Is QMS, interpreted over metric models, expressively complete for $L^2$? That is, is it the case that for every $L^2$-formula $\varphi(x)$ with one free point variable, there exists a QMS-formula $\psi$ such that, in every metric model, $\varphi(x)$ and $\psi$ are satisfied at exactly the same points?

Let $\mathbb{N}=\{1,2,3,\dots\}$. Given a finite set $F=\{\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_k\}$ of affine functions $\alpha_i:\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}\to\mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}$ of the form $\alpha_i(x)=m_i x+a_i$ with integers $m_i\ge 2$ and $a_i\ge 0$, and given a nonnegative integer $z$, let $E[F:z]\subseteq\{0\}\cup\mathbb{N}$ denote the smallest set containing $z$ and closed under each $\alpha_i\in F$. The (upper) asymptotic density of a set $S\subseteq\{0\}\cup\mathbb{N}$ is $\limsup_{N\to\infty}\frac{|S\cap[1,N]|}{N}$.

For each of the following six choices of $F=\{\alpha_1,\alpha_2,\alpha_3\}$, prove or disprove that the asymptotic density of $E[F:0]$ equals $0$:

(1) $\alpha_1(x)=2x,\ \alpha_2(x)=3x+3,\ \alpha_3(x)=6x+10$;
(2) $\alpha_1(x)=2x,\ \alpha_2(x)=6x+3,\ \alpha_3(x)=3x+2$;
(3) $\alpha_1(x)=3x,\ \alpha_2(x)=2x+2,\ \alpha_3(x)=6x+15$;
(4) $\alpha_1(x)=3x,\ \alpha_2(x)=6x+2,\ \alpha_3(x)=2x+1$;
(5) $\alpha_1(x)=6x,\ \alpha_2(x)=3x+1,\ \alpha_3(x)=2x+2$;
(6) $\alpha_1(x)=6x,\ \alpha_2(x)=2x+1,\ \alpha_3(x)=3x+6$.

Let S = ([0,1], *, ≤) be an interval semigroup, meaning that * : [0,1]² → [0,1] is an associative, commutative, monotone (with respect to the usual order ≤) binary operation. Assume that:

1. * is continuous on [0,1]²;
2. 0 is an annihilator, i.e. x * 0 = 0 for all x ∈ [0,1];
3. S has no neutral element;
4. S is Archimedean, i.e. for every x, y ∈ (0,1) there exists n ∈ ℕ such that x^n := x * x * ⋯ * x (n times) satisfies x^n < y;
5. S is nilpotent, i.e. for every x ∈ [0,1) there exists n ∈ ℕ with x^n = 0.

A function f : [0,1] → [0,+∞] is called a multiplicative generator of * if it is monotone and one-to-one on its support, and 
   x * y = f^{(-1)}(f(x) · f(y))   for all x, y ∈ [0,1],
where f^{(-1)} denotes a suitable pseudo-inverse of f and · is the usual product of real numbers.

Characterize precisely those nilpotent interval semigroups S (in the above sense) that admit only non-continuous multiplicative generators, i.e. for which no continuous multiplicative generator f exists.

Let $\mu_0$ and $\mu_1$ be two positive Borel measures on the real line with finite moments. Consider the Sobolev inner product
$$\langle f,g\rangle_S \;=\; \int f(x)\,g(x)\,d\mu_0(x) \;+\; \int f'(x)\,g'(x)\,d\mu_1(x),$$
and let $\{Q_n\}_{n\ge 0}$ denote the sequence of monic polynomials orthogonal with respect to $\langle\cdot,\cdot\rangle_S$.

The asymptotic behavior of $Q_n$ (outer relative asymptotics with respect to the standard orthogonal polynomials for $\mu_0$, Mehler–Heine type asymptotics, and asymptotic zero distribution) is known in the case where $\operatorname{supp}(\mu_1)\subseteq\operatorname{supp}(\mu_0)$ is a compact set, by means of algebraic identities available for the (discrete or continuous) Sobolev product in that nested situation.

Determine the asymptotic behavior of $\{Q_n\}$ in the following non-nested configurations, where those algebraic identities are not available:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $\operatorname{supp}(\mu_0)\cap\operatorname{supp}(\mu_1)=\emptyset$ (disjoint supports);
\item[(b)] $\operatorname{supp}(\mu_0)\cap\operatorname{supp}(\mu_1)\neq\emptyset$, but neither support contains the other (overlapping but non-nested supports).
\end{enumerate}
In each case, establish (i) the outer relative asymptotics of $Q_n(z)$ off the supports, (ii) Mehler–Heine type local asymptotics, and (iii) the limiting distribution of the zeros of $Q_n$.

Let $n \geq 3$ and let $S$ be an $n$-simplex in Euclidean space $\mathbb{E}^n$, that is, the convex hull of $n+1$ affinely independent points. Define:
- the centroid $G$ of $S$ as the arithmetic mean of its $n+1$ vertices;
- the circumcenter $C$ of $S$ as the unique point equidistant from all $n+1$ vertices (the center of the circumscribed sphere);
- the incenter $I$ of $S$ as the unique point in the interior of $S$ equidistant from all $n+1$ facet hyperplanes (the center of the inscribed sphere);
- the Euler line of $S$ as the affine hull of $C$ and $G$ (a line, provided $C \neq G$).

Give a purely geometric characterization of those $n$-simplices $S \subset \mathbb{E}^n$ (for each $n \geq 3$) for which the incenter $I$ lies on the Euler line of $S$.

(For comparison, in the planar case $n = 2$, a triangle has its incenter on its Euler line if and only if it is isosceles; the problem asks for the analogous characterization in dimension $n \geq 3$.)

Let $X$ be an affine scheme over an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic zero, with coordinate ring $A = k[X]$. Let $\mathfrak{g}$ be a finite-dimensional Lie algebra (the target algebra), and let $\Gamma$ be a finite group acting by automorphisms on both $X$ (hence on $A$) and on $\mathfrak{g}$. Form the Lie algebra $\mathfrak{g} \otimes A$ with the pointwise bracket $[u \otimes a, v \otimes b] = [u,v] \otimes ab$, and let $\Gamma$ act diagonally. The associated equivariant map algebra is
$$M = M(X, \mathfrak{g})^{\Gamma} = (\mathfrak{g} \otimes A)^{\Gamma},$$
the fixed-point subalgebra under this diagonal action.

While the category of finite-dimensional $M$-modules has been studied extensively, develop a representation theory of $M$ beyond the finite-dimensional setting. Specifically, define and investigate natural infinite-dimensional module categories for an arbitrary equivariant map algebra $M$, including:

(a) an appropriate analogue of the Bernstein–Gelfand–Gelfand category $\mathcal{O}$;
(b) a category of integrable highest-weight representations;
(c) a category of quasifinite representations, namely weight modules whose weight spaces are all finite-dimensional;
(d) other suitable infinite-dimensional module categories generalizing those known for loop algebras, current algebras, and affine Kac–Moody algebras.

For each such category, classify the irreducible objects, describe block decompositions, character/extension structure, and identify when classical features (e.g., highest-weight theory, BGG duality, Weyl modules) admit analogues in the general equivariant map algebra setting.

Setup. An (abstract) argumentation framework is a pair F = (A, R) where A is a finite set of arguments and R ⊆ A × A is an attack relation. For S ⊆ A:
- S is conflict-free if there are no a, b ∈ S with (a, b) ∈ R;
- S is admissible if it is conflict-free and for every b ∈ A with (b, a) ∈ R for some a ∈ S, there exists c ∈ S with (c, b) ∈ R;
- S is a preferred extension if it is a ⊆-maximal admissible set;
- S is a stable extension if it is conflict-free and for every b ∈ A \ S there is a ∈ S with (a, b) ∈ R;
- S is a complete extension if it is admissible and contains every argument it defends;
- S is a semi-stable extension if it is a complete extension whose range S ∪ {b : ∃ a ∈ S, (a, b) ∈ R} is ⊆-maximal among complete extensions.

For a semantics σ ∈ {st, ss, pr} (stable, semi-stable, preferred), a framework F, and a target set E ⊆ A, define the σ-characteristic char_σ(F, E) under the "update" modification type U — which allows arbitrary modifications of the attack relation R (adding or removing any attacks, on the same set of arguments A) — as the minimum number of elementary modifications needed to transform F into a framework F' such that E is contained in some σ-extension of F' (with char_σ(F, E) = ∞ if no such F' exists, and otherwise a nonnegative integer).

The spectrum is
   S(st, ss, pr, U) = { (k, l, m) ∈ (ℕ ∪ {∞})³ : ∃ (F, E) with char_st(F, E) = k, char_ss(F, E) = l, char_pr(F, E) = m }.

A triple (k, l, m) is called monotonically decreasing if k ≥ l ≥ m. Call the set of all monotonically decreasing triples the m.d.s.-complete set.

Problem. Determine S(st, ss, pr, U) exactly. In particular, decide whether S(st, ss, pr, U) is m.d.s.-complete, i.e. whether
   S(st, ss, pr, U) = { (k, l, m) : k ≥ l ≥ m }.

Let $n \geq 2$. An entire function $F:\mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}$ is said to have bounded $L$-index in a direction $\mathbf{b} \in \mathbb{C}^n \setminus \{0\}$ on a set $G \subseteq \mathbb{C}^n$, where $L:\mathbb{C}^n \to (0,\infty)$ is a positive continuous function, if there exists a nonnegative integer $m_0$ such that for every $z \in G$,
$$ \frac{1}{m!\, L(z)^{m}} \left| \partial^{m}_{\mathbf{b}} F(z) \right| \;\le\; \max_{0 \le k \le m_0}\, \frac{1}{k!\, L(z)^{k}} \left| \partial^{k}_{\mathbf{b}} F(z) \right| \qquad \text{for all } m \in \mathbb{Z}_{\ge 0}, $$
where $\partial^{k}_{\mathbf{b}} F(z) = \frac{d^{k}}{dt^{k}} F(z+t\mathbf{b}) \big|_{t=0}$ denotes the $k$-th directional derivative of $F$ along $\mathbf{b}$.

Construct (or prove the impossibility of constructing) the following data:
- an entire function $F:\mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}$;
- a positive continuous function $L:\mathbb{C}^n \to (0,\infty)$;
- two disjoint unbounded domains $G_1, G_2 \subseteq \mathbb{C}^n$ with $G_1 \cup G_2 = \mathbb{C}^n$;
- two directions $\mathbf{b}_1, \mathbf{b}_2 \in \mathbb{C}^n \setminus \{0\}$;

such that $F$ has bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_1$ on $G_1$ and bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_2$ on $G_2$, but $F$ does not have bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_2$ on $G_1$ and does not have bounded $L$-index in direction $\mathbf{b}_1$ on $G_2$ (i.e., no single integer $m_0$ makes the corresponding inequality hold throughout $G_1$ with direction $\mathbf{b}_2$, nor throughout $G_2$ with direction $\mathbf{b}_1$).

Let G = (V(G), E(G)) be a finite simple undirected graph. For a positive integer m, G is called m-connected if |V(G)| > m and G remains connected after the removal of any set of fewer than m vertices. For S ⊆ V(G), G[S] denotes the subgraph induced by S, and a partition (S, T) of V(G) means S ∪ T = V(G), S ∩ T = ∅, with both S and T nonempty.

Prove or disprove: For every positive integer ℓ, there exists a positive integer k = k(ℓ) such that for every k-connected graph G and every subset X ⊆ V(G) with |X| ≤ ℓ, there is a partition (S, T) of V(G) satisfying all of the following:
  (i) X ⊆ S;
  (ii) every vertex of S has at least ℓ neighbors in T;
  (iii) the induced subgraph G[S] is ℓ-connected;
  (iv) the induced subgraph G[T] is ℓ-connected.

Let $I$ be an infinite set and let $D$ be a regular ultrafilter on $I$. (Recall that $D$ is regular if there exists a family $\{X_i : i \in I\} \subseteq D$ such that every infinite subfamily has empty intersection.) Consider the linear order $(\omega,<)^I/D$, the reduced power of the natural numbers modulo $D$.

For a pair of regular cardinals $(\kappa,\mu)$ with $\kappa,\mu \le |I|$, say that $(\kappa,\mu)$ is a cut of $(\omega,<)^I/D$ if there exist sequences $(a_\alpha)_{\alpha<\kappa}$ and $(b_\beta)_{\beta<\mu}$ in $(\omega,<)^I/D$ such that:
(i) $a_\alpha < a_{\alpha'}$ whenever $\alpha < \alpha' < \kappa$;
(ii) $b_{\beta'} < b_\beta$ whenever $\beta < \beta' < \mu$;
(iii) $a_\alpha < b_\beta$ for all $\alpha<\kappa$, $\beta<\mu$;
(iv) there is no $c$ in $(\omega,<)^I/D$ satisfying $a_\alpha < c < b_\beta$ for all $\alpha,\beta$.
The cut spectrum $C(D)$ is the set of all such pairs $(\kappa,\mu)$. A cut $(\kappa,\mu)$ is symmetric if $\kappa = \mu$, and asymmetric otherwise.

Assume $C(D) \neq \emptyset$, and let $\kappa$ be the least regular cardinal $\le |I|$ for which the symmetric cut $(\kappa,\kappa)$ lies in $C(D)$. Determine the set of regular cardinals $\mu < \kappa$ for which the asymmetric cut $(\kappa,\mu)$ can also belong to $C(D)$.

Background. A vertex operator algebra (VOA) is a Z-graded vector space V = ⊕_{n∈Z} V_{(n)} (with dim V_{(n)} < ∞ and V_{(n)} = 0 for n sufficiently negative) equipped with a vacuum vector 1, a conformal vector ω, and a vertex operator map Y(·,x): V → (End V)[[x,x^{-1}]] satisfying the standard axioms (vacuum, creation, Jacobi identity / weak commutativity and associativity, Virasoro relations coming from Y(ω,x)). A meromorphic open-string vertex algebra (MOSVA) is a noncommutative generalization of a VOA: it is a Z-graded vector space with grading-restriction conditions, a vacuum, a conformal vector and a vertex operator map Y(·,x) satisfying the vacuum property, the d/dx-derivative property, the Virasoro relations, and a meromorphic associativity axiom, but not requiring the (skew-)symmetry that holds for a VOA. Every VOA V is in particular a MOSVA. A V-bimodule W for V regarded as a MOSVA is a graded vector space equipped with a left and a right vertex operator action of V which together satisfy meromorphic associativity. 

For V regarded as a MOSVA and for a V-bimodule W there is a cohomology theory H^n(V, W) (n ∈ Z_+) defined analogously to Hochschild cohomology of associative algebras, constructed from cochains given by certain rational-function-valued multilinear maps that are required to satisfy suitable convergence and compatibility (composability) conditions.

Problem. Let V be a vertex operator algebra of one of the following types:
(a) a Wess–Zumino–Witten model VOA L(k,0) associated to an affine Kac–Moody algebra at positive integer level;
(b) a minimal model VOA (the simple Virasoro VOA L(c_{p,q},0) at a discrete-series central charge);
(c) a lattice VOA V_L associated to a positive-definite even lattice L;
(d) the moonshine module vertex operator algebra V^♮.

Prove or disprove: for every such V, every V-bimodule W (where V is viewed as a meromorphic open-string vertex algebra), and every positive integer n, the cohomology group H^n(V, W) vanishes.

Let V = ⨁_{n≥0} V_{(n)} be a simple ℤ-graded vertex operator algebra over ℂ satisfying:
(i) V_{(0)} = ℂ·𝟙 (the vacuum is the unique, up to scalar, lowest-weight vector);
(ii) V_{(n)} = 0 for n < 0;
(iii) V is isomorphic to its contragredient module V′ as a V-module;
(iv) V is C_2-cofinite, i.e. dim V/C_2(V) < ∞ where C_2(V) = span{u_{-2}v : u,v ∈ V}.

Under these hypotheses, the theory of (possibly logarithmic) intertwining operators among generalized V-modules produces, via associativity and modular invariance, genus-zero and genus-one chiral correlation functions valued in multivalued analytic functions on configuration spaces of marked points on the Riemann sphere and on the torus, with logarithmic singularities permitted.

Question (two parts):
(a) Do there exist genus-zero and genus-one full (non-chiral) correlation functions for a logarithmic full conformal field theory associated with V, constructible from the genus-zero and genus-one chiral correlation functions arising from V and its module category (so that the resulting full correlation functions are single-valued on the corresponding moduli spaces and compatible with operator product expansion and the modular action)?

(b) If so, can one assemble these genus-zero and genus-one full correlation functions into a two-dimensional conformal field theory in the sense of Kontsevich and Segal — i.e. a symmetric monoidal functor from the category of Riemann surfaces with parametrized boundary to the category of (suitable topological) vector spaces, satisfying all of the Kontsevich–Segal axioms with the exception of those axioms involving unitarity (positivity of the inner product / reflection positivity)?

Let $V = \bigoplus_{n \in \mathbb{Z}} V_{(n)}$ be a simple vertex operator algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying:
(i) $V_{(0)} = \mathbb{C}\mathbf{1}$ and $V_{(n)} = 0$ for $n < 0$;
(ii) $V$ is $C_2$-cofinite, i.e., $\dim V/C_2(V) < \infty$, where $C_2(V) = \mathrm{span}\{u_{-2}v : u,v \in V\}$;
(iii) the contragredient module $V'$ is isomorphic to $V$ as a $V$-module;
(iv) every grading-restricted generalized $V$-module is completely reducible.

Let $G$ be a finite group of automorphisms of $V$. For each $g \in G$, let $\mathcal{C}_g$ denote the category of grading-restricted generalized $g$-twisted $V$-modules, and let
$$\mathcal{C} = \bigoplus_{g \in G} \mathcal{C}_g.$$

Recall that a $G$-crossed (braided) tensor category in the sense of Turaev is a tensor category $\mathcal{C}$ equipped with a decomposition $\mathcal{C} = \bigoplus_{g \in G}\mathcal{C}_g$, a monoidal $G$-action $\varphi: G \to \mathrm{Aut}_\otimes(\mathcal{C})$ such that $\varphi_h(\mathcal{C}_g) \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{hgh^{-1}}$, tensor products satisfying $\mathcal{C}_g \otimes \mathcal{C}_h \subseteq \mathcal{C}_{gh}$, and a $G$-braiding $c_{X,Y}: X \otimes Y \to \varphi_g(Y) \otimes X$ for $X \in \mathcal{C}_g$ satisfying the appropriate hexagon axioms.

Question: Does $\mathcal{C} = \bigoplus_{g \in G}\mathcal{C}_g$ carry the structure of a $G$-crossed (braided) tensor category in the sense of Turaev?

Let $V = \bigoplus_{n \geq 0} V_n$ be a vertex operator algebra over $\mathbb{C}$ satisfying:
(1) the central charge of $V$ is $24$;
(2) every irreducible $V$-module is isomorphic to $V$ itself (i.e., $V$ is holomorphic / self-dual);
(3) every $V$-module is completely reducible (i.e., $V$ is rational).

The weight-one homogeneous subspace $V_1$ carries the structure of a finite-dimensional Lie algebra under the bracket $[a,b] := a_{(0)}b$ for $a,b \in V_1$.

Question: Is such a $V$ determined uniquely (up to isomorphism of vertex operator algebras) by the isomorphism type of the Lie algebra $V_1$?

Let β > 1 be a Pisot number, that is, a real algebraic integer greater than 1 all of whose Galois conjugates (other than β itself) lie strictly inside the open unit disk in the complex plane. Set
   B = {0, ±1, ±2, …, ±⌊β⌋} ⊂ ℤ,
where ⌊·⌋ denotes the floor function. Let
   ℤ[1/β] = { Σ_{i=−N}^{M} a_i β^{i} : N, M ∈ ℤ_{≥0}, a_i ∈ ℤ }
be the ring generated by 1/β over ℤ, and let
   B[1/β] = { Σ_{i=−N}^{M} b_i β^{i} : N, M ∈ ℤ_{≥0}, b_i ∈ B }
be the set of all such expressions whose digits are restricted to lie in B. Prove or disprove that
   ℤ[1/β] ∩ [0, 1) = B[1/β] ∩ [0, 1);
equivalently, show that every nonnegative element of ℤ[1/β] in the unit interval admits a finite representation Σ_{i ≥ 1} b_i β^{−i} with all digits b_i ∈ B.

There are 34 positive integers n ≤ 20000 for which the number of isomorphism types of groups of order n has been determined but a complete, explicit list of representative groups (one for each isomorphism type) is not available. These orders are:

(i) n = 2^10 = 1024;
(ii) the 32 orders of the following forms:
   • 2^9 · p, where p is an odd prime with 2^9 · p ≤ 20000;
   • 2^8 · p · q, where p ∈ {3, 5, 7} and q is a prime with q ∉ {2, p} and 2^8 · p · q ≤ 20000;
   • 2^7 · 3^2 · p, where p is a prime with p ∉ {2, 3} and 2^7 · 3^2 · p ≤ 20000;
   • the specific orders 8640, 9600, 13440, 16320, 17280, 19440;
(iii) n = 12500.

For each such order n, explicitly construct a complete and irredundant list of finite groups of order n, that is, exhibit (e.g. via presentations, polycyclic generating sequences, or some other explicit description) one representative from every isomorphism class of groups of order n.

Let G be an abelian Hausdorff topological group. The dual group G^ of G is the group of all continuous homomorphisms from G into the circle group T = R/Z, equipped with the compact-open topology. The second dual G^^ is defined analogously as the dual of G^. There is a canonical homomorphism α_G : G → G^^ given by α_G(x)(χ) = χ(x). The group G is called reflexive if α_G is a topological group isomorphism (i.e. an algebraic isomorphism that is also a homeomorphism). A topological group H is called separable if it has a countable dense subset, and metrizable if its topology is induced by a metric. A quotient group of G is the group G/N equipped with the quotient topology, where N is a closed normal subgroup of G.

Question. Does every infinite reflexive abelian topological group G admit a Hausdorff quotient group G/N that is:
(i) non-trivial and separable;
(ii) infinite and separable;
(iii) separable and metrizable;
(iv) infinite, separable, and metrizable?

Consider a semiclassical black hole of initial ADM mass $M_{BH}$ that undergoes Hawking evaporation. In a "remnant" scenario, evaporation does not proceed to complete evanescence: it halts either when the mass approaches the Planck mass $m_P=\sqrt{\hbar c/G}$, or when it approaches some intermediate remnant mass scale $M_r$ with $m_P \ll M_r \ll M_{BH}$, leaving behind an eternal or extremely long-lived localized object. The remnant is required to:

(i) preserve unitarity of the full evaporation process by either storing all of the information that fell into the original black hole or releasing it on arbitrarily long timescales;
(ii) shield the curvature singularity from external observers, so that no naked singularity is seen at any stage of evaporation, including near $m_P$;
(iii) carry an entropy at least equal to the initial Bekenstein–Hawking entropy $S_{BH}=A/(4\ell_P^2)$ of the original black hole, while having a localized spatial extent of order at most the Planck (or remnant) length.

Determine whether there exists a consistent semiclassical/quantum-gravitational model of such a remnant which simultaneously satisfies (i)–(iii) without:

(a) violating the Bekenstein entropy bound $S \le 2\pi k_B R E/(\hbar c)$ for a system of effective energy $E$ and radius $R$;
(b) producing pathologies such as an infinite species problem or unbounded pair-production of remnants in low-energy processes;
(c) conflicting with the holographic counting of microstates expected from the AdS/CFT correspondence for asymptotically AdS black holes.

Either construct such a model and prove its consistency with (a)–(c), or prove that no such remnant model can exist.

A Regge spacetime is a four-dimensional piecewise linear (PL) manifold equipped with a piecewise linear Lorentzian metric, specified combinatorially by assigning (squared) edge lengths to the 1-simplices of a simplicial triangulation; on each 4-simplex the metric is taken to be flat, and curvature is concentrated as deficit angles on the 2-simplices ("bones"). The Regge equations of motion are the discrete analogue of the vacuum Einstein equations, obtained by varying the Regge action with respect to the edge lengths.

(a) Formulate and prove a precise sense in which a sequence of Regge spacetimes (with edge lengths satisfying the vacuum Regge equations and with mesh size tending to zero, under suitable non-degeneracy or "fatness" conditions on the simplices) converges to a smooth vacuum solution of the Einstein equations Ric(g) = 0. In particular, identify:
  (i) the topology or function space in which the PL metrics, their associated curvature measures, and the Regge action converge to their smooth counterparts;
  (ii) hypotheses on the triangulation (regularity, shape-regularity, causal structure) that guarantee such convergence;
  (iii) error estimates as a function of mesh parameters.

(b) Formulate the analogous convergence question for spacetimes produced by numerical relativity: given a numerical scheme that produces approximate solutions to the vacuum Einstein equations on a discretized spacetime (e.g., finite-difference, finite-element, or spectral discretizations of an evolution formulation such as BSSN or generalized harmonic gauge), state precisely in what sense the discrete approximations converge to a smooth vacuum spacetime as the discretization is refined, including the function space of convergence, required regularity of the limit, and rates of convergence.

In applied information geometry, a data object (a "complex system" or "Big Data" set) is typically modeled by a (possibly singular) statistical manifold: a measurable family of probability distributions $\{p_\theta : \theta \in \Theta\}$ on some sample space, together with the Fisher information metric $g_F$ and a dually flat pair of affine connections $(\nabla^{(\alpha)}, \nabla^{(-\alpha)})$ arising from the Amari $\alpha$-family, when these structures exist. By a "prescribed geometric structure" we mean a specified tuple such as a Riemannian metric, a Hessian (dually flat) structure, a Kähler structure, a contact or symplectic structure, or a foliation of prescribed codimension and holonomy. By a "prescribed topological structure" we mean a specified homotopy/homeomorphism/diffeomorphism type, a prescribed CW or simplicial decomposition, a prescribed sheaf-cohomological invariant, or a prescribed (persistent) homology profile.

Problem. Develop effective criteria, and where possible an algorithm, that take as input a complex system or data object $X$ (presented either as an empirical distribution, a parameterized statistical model, or a finite metric/measure space) and a prescribed geometric structure $G$ or prescribed topological structure $T$, and decide:

(a) whether $X$ admits a representation as a statistical manifold (or stratified statistical space) carrying the structure $G$;

(b) whether the underlying space of $X$ admits the topological structure $T$;

(c) when such structures exist, classify them up to the natural equivalence (isometry, affine equivalence, diffeomorphism, homotopy equivalence).

In particular, identify obstructions (curvature, characteristic classes, cohomological invariants, entropy/divergence-based invariants) whose vanishing is necessary and sufficient for the existence of $G$ or $T$ on $X$.

Let F be a field and let V = (V^+, V^-) be a Jordan pair over F. Suppose V carries a fully idempotent root grading R, meaning there is a 3-graded locally finite root system R with set of "1-roots" R_1 and a decomposition V^σ = ⊕_{α∈R_1} V_α^σ (σ ∈ {+,-}) compatible with the Jordan pair structure, in which each root space is generated by idempotents in the appropriate sense. Assume further that this grading is a division grading: every root space V_α = (V_α^+, V_α^-) is a Jordan division pair, i.e. for every σ and every nonzero x ∈ V_α^σ, the quadratic operator Q(x) : V_α^{-σ} → V_α^σ is invertible. Let St(V, R) denote the Steinberg group associated to (V, R), defined by the standard generators and Steinberg-type relations indexed by R.

Prove or disprove: if 2 ≤ rank R ≤ 4 and St(V, R) is not centrally closed (i.e. does not coincide with its universal central extension within the relevant category), then necessarily dim_F V_α = 1 for every α ∈ R_1, and moreover the pair (type of (R, R_1), |F|) belongs to the following finite list of exceptional cases:
- (A_1^2, 2), (A_1^2, 4);
- (A_1^3, 2), (A_2^3, 2);
- (C_2^her, 2);
- (B_3^qf, 3);
- (C_3^her, 2);
- (D_4^alt, 2).

Consider a multiobjective optimization problem of the form: minimize F(x) = (f_1(x), f_2(x), ..., f_k(x)) subject to x ∈ S ⊆ R^n, where k ≥ 2 and each f_i: S → R. The goal is to approximate the Pareto-optimal set P* = { x ∈ S : there is no y ∈ S with f_i(y) ≤ f_i(x) for all i and f_j(y) < f_j(x) for some j } and the corresponding Pareto front F(P*).

A multiobjective evolutionary algorithm (MOEA) is a population-based stochastic search procedure maintaining a population P_t ⊂ S that evolves via variation operators (e.g., recombination and mutation) and selection. Classical mathematical programming techniques for multiobjective problems include:
 (i) the ε-constraint method, which optimizes one objective f_j while imposing f_i(x) ≤ ε_i for i ≠ j;
 (ii) min-max (Chebyshev) scalarizations of the form min_{x ∈ S} max_i w_i (f_i(x) − z_i*), where z* is a reference point and w_i ≥ 0 are weights;
 (iii) game-theoretic formulations in which each objective is assigned to a player and Nash or Stackelberg equilibria of the resulting game are sought.

Problem. Devise a principled hybridization scheme that couples an MOEA with one or more of the techniques (i)–(iii) such that the resulting algorithm provably improves upon the pure MOEA in at least one of the following senses, and characterize the conditions on F and S under which such an improvement holds:
 (a) convergence rate to the Pareto front under a chosen performance indicator (e.g., hypervolume, inverted generational distance, ε-indicator);
 (b) approximation quality of the produced finite archive to P*;
 (c) computational complexity (number of function evaluations) required to reach a prescribed indicator value with prescribed probability.

Formally specify the hybrid algorithm, identify the assumptions on F (continuity, convexity, smoothness, Lipschitz constants, etc.) and on S, and establish rigorous convergence and/or complexity guarantees that demonstrate strict improvement over the corresponding non-hybrid baseline.

Call a function $f$ on a domain $\Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^2$ (or on a two-dimensional manifold) *partially analytic* if every level set $\{f = c\}$ is a real analytic curve, while $f$ itself is only required to have finite Sobolev regularity $H^s$ transversally to its level sets. The collection $\mathcal{P}(\Omega)$ of partially analytic functions is not closed under pointwise addition: the sum of two partially analytic functions generically fails to have real analytic level sets.

Identify a natural mathematical structure (for example, a groupoid, a category, a sheaf-theoretic or fibered structure, a non-linear topological space, or another suitable algebraic/geometric framework) that should replace the vector-space structure on $\mathcal{P}(\Omega)$, in such a way that:

(i) the structure restricts to the usual vector-space structure on subspaces of fully real analytic functions;
(ii) the structure is preserved by area-preserving diffeomorphisms acting by pullback (so that it is compatible with the symplectic / Hamiltonian setting of two-dimensional ideal hydrodynamics);
(iii) the structure interacts naturally with the Sobolev regularity profile across levels, allowing one to formulate well-posedness statements for two-dimensional incompressible Euler-type equations whose vorticity (or stream function) is partially analytic.

Equivalently: construct the correct ambient "space" of partially analytic functions and describe its operations.

Let $S^{2m+1}\subset\mathbb{R}^{2m+2}$ be the unit sphere of odd dimension $2m+1$, $m\ge 1$, with its standard round metric. A unit vector field $V$ on $S^{2m+1}$ has volume
$$\mathrm{vol}(V)=\int_{S^{2m+1}}\sqrt{\det(I+(\nabla V)^{t}\nabla V)}\,d\mathrm{vol},$$
where $\nabla$ is the Levi-Civita connection. A Hopf vector field $H$ on $S^{2m+1}$ is a unit Killing vector field tangent to the fibers of a Hopf fibration; equivalently, $H_p=Jp$ for a complex structure $J$ on $\mathbb{R}^{2m+2}$ compatible with the inner product.

Fix an integer $r$ with $1\le r\le m$ (so that, after a unitary change of coordinates, one may split $\mathbb{R}^{2m+2}=\mathbb{C}^{r+1}\oplus\mathbb{C}^{m-r}$). Consider a smooth one-parameter family $\{V_t\}_{t\in[0,1]}$ of unit vector fields on $S^{2m+1}$ such that:
\begin{itemize}
\item $V_0=H$ is a Hopf vector field;
\item $V_1$ is a parallel-transport unit vector field on $S^{2m+1}$ (a unit vector field obtained by parallel translation along great circles from a fixed point, defined on the complement of the antipode and continuously extended);
\item the family interpolates between $V_0$ and $V_1$ in the standard way associated with the index $r$, so that the volume function $t\mapsto \mathrm{vol}(V_t)$ satisfies $\frac{d}{dt}\mathrm{vol}(V_t)\big|_{t=0^+}<0$ (volume decreases for small $t>0$).
\end{itemize}

Determine all pairs $(m,r)$ with $m\ge 1$, $1\le r\le m$, for which the function $t\mapsto\mathrm{vol}(V_t)$ is strictly decreasing on the entire interval $[0,1]$ (not merely for small $t>0$).
